INT. HAYDEN PLANETARIUM DOME - NIGHT (BLACKOUT)

The star projector is dead. Only a handful of emergency lights glow along the wall. The dome ceiling is a flat gray void.

INSPECTOR AGNES TRELAWNEY, fifties, in a belted raincoat, stands at the center aisle with a torch. PROFESSOR BALTHAZAR OYELARAN, seventies, in a velvet jacket, sits in the front row. CORMAC, a lanky security guard, hovers by the exit. ASTRID VOLKOVA, thirties, the curator, wrings her hands beside the projector console.

                    AGNES
          Who last saw Doctor Pell alive?

                    ASTRID
          I did, at nine. He went to calibrate the

                    BALTHAZAR
          He was fussing with the Zeiss, as usual.

                    CORMAC
          I locked the east door at ten. Nobody came in.

                    AGNES
          Nobody went out?

                    CORMAC
          Nobody.

Her radio crackles.

                    PUI-LING (O.S.)
          Inspector, Sergeant Pui-Ling. We have the body in the sub-basement. Cause of death looks like electrical.

                    AGNES
          (into radio)
          Understood. Keep the scene sealed.

                    BALTHAZAR
          Electrical? In a planetarium. How apt.

                    ASTRID
          Professor, please.

                    BALTHAZAR
          Please what? A man is dead, and the stars are out. It's a metaphor, my dear.

                    CORMAC
          Sir, it's a crime scene.

                    AGNES
          Quiet, all of you.

A door clatters open at the far end of the dome. JAMAL REYES, twenties, in a janitor's coverall, stumbles in with a bucket, blinking at the torchlight. He sees four faces turned toward him and freezes.

                    JAMAL
          Sorry. Nobody told me there was a meeting.

                    ASTRID
          Jamal, where have you been?

                    JAMAL
          Mopping the lobby. The power went out, I thought

                    AGNES
          Mr. Reyes. Put down the bucket.

He does. A little water slops onto the floor and spreads across the tiles, catching the emergency light in a thin, bright line.

                    PUI-LING (O.S.)
          Inspector, there's a mop in the sub-basement. Wet.

Agnes looks at Jamal. Jamal looks at the bucket. Cormac steps in front of the exit.
